# Line endings are part of the integrity story: corpus CSVs, manifests, frozen Stage 3 data and a few frozen source files are pinned by sha256 of their
# exact BYTES (src/retrieval/corpus.py, scripts/stage3c2_freeze_v2.py, scripts/check_integrity.py). Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which
# would check these files out with CRLF and make every fresh Windows clone (and the windows-latest CI job) fail the integrity check.
# Forcing LF in the working tree on every OS keeps the bytes identical to what was hashed.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows launchers need CRLF to be reliable in cmd.exe / Windows PowerShell 5.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Never touched by line-ending conversion.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.pdf binary
*.pt binary
*.pth binary
*.faiss binary
*.npy binary
*.npz binary
*.safetensors binary
*.zip binary
*.db binary

# Stage 7B: these files are hash-pinned over their exact bytes AND are stored with Windows (CRLF) line endings (they were written by Python on Windows, and their
# checksums were computed over those bytes). Converting them to LF, as the rule above would, changes the bytes and breaks the pinned checksums. "-text" = byte-exact in
# both directions on every OS. tests/mlops/test_publication_safety.py finds any pinned CRLF file that is missing from this list.
data/retrieval/eval/immutable_pre_pooling_stage2b/retrieval_benchmark_minilm.json -text
data/retrieval/eval/immutable_pre_pooling_stage2b/retrieval_benchmark_minilm.md -text
